				<div class=3D"entry-content wp-block-post-content is-layout-flow wp-blo=
ck-post-content-is-layout-flow"><h2 id=3D"overview" class=3D"is-toc-heading=
" tabindex=3D"-1"><a href=3D"https://developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/exten=
ding-the-rest-api/schema/#overview">Overview</a></h2>
<p>A schema is metadata that tells us how our data is structured. Most data=
bases implement some form of schema which enables us to reason about our da=
ta in a more structured manner. The WordPress REST API utilizes <a href=3D"=
http://json-schema.org/">JSON Schema</a> to handle the structuring of its d=
ata. You can implement endpoints without using a schema, but you will be mi=
ssing out on a lot of things. It is up to you to decide what suits you best=
.</p>
<h3 id=3D"json" class=3D"is-toc-heading" tabindex=3D"-1"><a href=3D"https:/=
/developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/extending-the-rest-api/schema/#json">JSON=
</a></h3>
<p>First, let=E2=80=99s talk about JSON a bit. JSON is a human readable dat=
a format that resembles JavaScript objects. JSON stands for JavaScript Obje=
ct Notation. JSON is growing wildly in popularity and seems to be taking th=
e world of data structure by storm. The WordPress REST API uses a special s=
pecification for JSON known as JSON schema. To learn more about JSON Schema=
 please check out the <a href=3D"http://json-schema.org/">JSON Schema websi=
te</a> and this <a href=3D"https://json-schema.org/understanding-json-schem=
a">easier to understand introduction to JSON Schema</a>. Schema affords us =
many benefits: improved testing, discoverability, and overall better struct=
ure. Let=E2=80=99s look at a JSON blob of data.</p>
<pre class=3D"language-js" tabindex=3D"0"><code class=3D"language-js"><span=
 class=3D"token punctuation">{</span>
    <span class=3D"token string-property property">"shouldBeArray"</span><s=
pan class=3D"token operator">:</span> <span class=3D"token string">'LOL def=
initely not an array'</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
    <span class=3D"token string-property property">"shouldBeInteger"</span>=
<span class=3D"token operator">:</span> <span class=3D"token punctuation">[=
</span><span class=3D"token string">'lolz'</span><span class=3D"token punct=
uation">,</span> <span class=3D"token string">'you'</span><span class=3D"to=
ken punctuation">,</span> <span class=3D"token string">'need'</span><span c=
lass=3D"token punctuation">,</span> <span class=3D"token string">'schema'</=
span><span class=3D"token punctuation">]</span><span class=3D"token punctua=
tion">,</span>
    <span class=3D"token string-property property">"shouldBeString"</span><=
span class=3D"token operator">:</span> <span class=3D"token number">1234567=
89</span>
<span class=3D"token punctuation">}</span>
</code></pre>
<p>A JSON parser will go through that data no problem and won=E2=80=99t com=
plain about anything, because it is valid JSON. The clients and servers kno=
w nothing about the data and what to expect they just see the JSON. By impl=
ementing schema we can actually simplify our codebase. Schema will help str=
ucture our data better so our applications can more easily reason about our=
 interactions with the WordPress REST API. The WordPress REST API does not =
force you to use schema, but it is encouraged. There are two ways in which =
schema data is incorporated into the API; schema for resources and schema f=
or our registered arguments.</p>
<h3 id=3D"resource-schema" class=3D"is-toc-heading" tabindex=3D"-1"><a href=
=3D"https://developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/extending-the-rest-api/schema/=
#resource-schema">Resource Schema</a></h3>
<p>The schema for a resource indicates what fields are present for a partic=
ular object. When we register our routes we can also specify the resource s=
chema for the route. Let=E2=80=99s look at what a simple comment schema mig=
ht look like in a PHP representation of JSON schema.</p>
