=3D"token operator">=3D</span> <span class=3D"token variable">$schema</span=
><span class=3D"token punctuation">;</span>

        <span class=3D"token keyword">return</span> <span class=3D"token va=
riable">$this</span><span class=3D"token operator">-&gt;</span><span class=
=3D"token function">add_additional_fields_schema</span><span class=3D"token=
 punctuation">(</span> <span class=3D"token variable">$this</span><span cla=
ss=3D"token operator">-&gt;</span><span class=3D"token property">schema</sp=
an> <span class=3D"token punctuation">)</span><span class=3D"token punctuat=
ion">;</span>
    <span class=3D"token punctuation">}</span>
</span></code></pre>
<p>This pattern was introduced into WordPress core in version 5.3 in <a hre=
f=3D"https://core.trac.wordpress.org/ticket/47871">#47871</a>, and yielded =
up to 40% speed improvement when generating some API responses.</p>
<h3 id=3D"schema-documents" class=3D"is-toc-heading" tabindex=3D"-1"><a hre=
f=3D"https://developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/extending-the-rest-api/schema=
/#schema-documents">Schema Documents</a></h3>
<p>A basic schema document consists of a few properties.</p>
<ul>
<li><code>$schema</code> A reference to a meta schema that describes the ve=
rsion of the spec the document is using.</li>
<li><code>title</code> The title of the schema. Normally this is a human re=
adable label, but in WordPress this is machine readable string . The posts =
endpoint, for example, has a title of =E2=80=98post=E2=80=99. Comments has =
a title of =E2=80=98comment=E2=80=99.</li>
<li><code>type</code> This refers to the type of the value being described.=
 This can be any one of the seven primitive types. In WordPress the top-lev=
el type will almost always be an <code>object</code>, even for collection e=
ndpoints that return an array of objects.</li>
<li><code>properties</code> A list of the known properties contained in the=
 object and their definitions. Each property definition itself is also a sc=
hema, but without the $schema top-level property, more accurately described=
 as a sub-schema.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"primitive-types" class=3D"is-toc-heading" tabindex=3D"-1"><a href=
=3D"https://developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/extending-the-rest-api/schema/=
#primitive-types">Primitive Types</a></h3>
<p>JSON Schema defines a list of seven allowed <a href=3D"https://tools.iet=
f.org/html/draft-zyp-json-schema-04#section-3.5">primitive types</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li><code>string</code> A string value.</li>
<li><code>null</code> A <code>null</code> value.</li>
<li><code>number</code> Any number. Decimals are allowed. Equivalent to a <=
a href=3D"https://www.php.net/float"><code>float</code> in PHP</a>.</li>
<li><code>integer</code> A number, but decimals or exponents are not allowe=
d.</li>
<li><code>boolean</code> A <code>true</code> or <code>false</code> value. <=
/li>
<li><code>array</code> A list of values. This is equivalent to a <a href=3D=
"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_O=
bjects/Array">JavaScript array</a>. In PHP this is referred to as a numeric=
al array, or an array without defined keys.</li>
<li><code>object</code> A map of keys to values. This is equivalent to a <a=
 href=3D"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/=
Operators/Object_initializer">JavaScript object</a>. In PHP this is referre=
d to as an <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associative_array">asso=
ciative array</a>, or an array with defined keys.  </li>
</ul>
<p>A value=E2=80=99s primitive type is specified using the <code>type</code=
> keyword. For example, this is how you would define a <code>string</code> =
value with JSON Schema.</p>
<pre class=3D"language-php" tabindex=3D"0"><code class=3D"language-php"><sp=
an class=3D"token php language-php"><span class=3D"token keyword">array</sp=
an><span class=3D"token punctuation">(</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'type'</span> <span c=
lass=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token string single-q=
uoted-string">'string'</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
<span class=3D"token punctuation">)</span><span class=3D"token punctuation"=
>;</span>
</span></code></pre>
<p>JSON Schema allows for defining values that can be multiple types. For e=
xample, this is how you would define a value that can be either a <code>str=
ing</code> or a <code>boolean</code>.</p>
<pre class=3D"language-php" tabindex=3D"0"><code class=3D"language-php"><sp=
an class=3D"token php language-php"><span class=3D"token keyword">array</sp=
an><span class=3D"token punctuation">(</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'type'</span> <span c=
lass=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token keyword">array<=
/span><span class=3D"token punctuation">(</span> <span class=3D"token strin=
g single-quoted-string">'boolean'</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,=
</span> <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'string'</span> <=
span class=3D"token punctuation">)</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">=
,</span> =20
<span class=3D"token punctuation">)</span><span class=3D"token punctuation"=
>;</span>
</span></code></pre>
<h4 id=3D"type-juggling" class=3D"is-toc-heading" tabindex=3D"-1"><a href=
=3D"https://developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/extending-the-rest-api/schema/=
#type-juggling">Type Juggling</a></h4>
<p>Because the WordPress REST API accepts <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Percent-encoding#The_application/x-www-form-urlencoded_type">URL fo=
rm encoded</a> data both in the <code>POST</code> body or as the query port=
ion of the URL, many primtive types perform type juggling to convert these =
string values into their proper native types.</p>
<ul>
<li><code>string</code> Only strings according to <a href=3D"https://www.ph=
p.net/is_string"><code>is_string</code></a> are allowed.</li>
<li><code>null</code> Only a properly typed <code>null</code> is accepted. =
This means that submitting a null value in the URL or as a URL form encoded=
 post body is not possible, a JSON request body must be used.</li>
<li><code>number</code> Floats, integers, and strings that pass <a href=3D"=
https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.is-numeric.php"><code>is_numeric</co=
de></a> are allowed. Values will be casted to a <code>float</code>.</li>
<li><code>integer</code> Integers or strings that can be <a href=3D"https:/=
/www.php.net/manual/en/language.types.float.php#language.types.float.castin=
g">casted to a <code>float</code></a> with a fractional part that is equiva=
lent to 0.</li>
<li><code>boolean</code> Booleans, the integers <code>0</code> and <code>1<=
/code>, or the strings <code>"0"</code>, <code>"1"</code>, <code>"false"</c=
ode>, and <code>"true"</code>. <code>0</code> is treated as <code>false</co=
de> and <code>1</code> is treated as <code>true</code>.</li>
<li><code>array</code> Numeric arrays according to <a href=3D"https://devel=
oper.wordpress.org/reference/functions/wp_is_numeric_array/"><code>wp_is_nu=
meric_array</code></a> or a string. If the string is comma separated it wil=
l be split into an array, otherwise it will be an array containing the stri=
ng value. For example: <code>"red,yellow"</code> becomes <code>array( "red"=
, "yellow" )</code> and  <code>"blue"</code> becomes <code>array( "blue" )<=
/code>.</li>
<li><code>object</code> An array, an <code>stdClass</code> object, an objec=
t implementing <code>JsonSerializable</code> or an empty string. Values wil=
l be converted to a native PHP array.</li>
</ul>
<p>When using multiple types, types will be evaluated in the order they are=
 specified. This can have an impact on the sanitized data received by your =
REST API endpoint. For instance, in the previous example, if the value subm=
itted was <code>"1"</code>, it would be sanitized to the boolean <code>true=
</code> value. However, if the order was flipped, the value would remain as=
 the string <code>"1"</code>.</p>

<div class=3D"wp-block-wporg-notice is-info-notice">
<div class=3D"wp-block-wporg-notice__icon"></div>
<div class=3D"wp-block-wporg-notice__content">The JSON Schema specification=
 allows for defining schemas without a <code>type</code> field. The WordPre=
ss implementation however requires a <code>type</code> to be defined, and w=
ill issue a <code>_doing_it_wrong</code> notice if a type is ommitted.</div=
></div>

<h3 id=3D"format" class=3D"is-toc-heading" tabindex=3D"-1"><a href=3D"https=
://developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/extending-the-rest-api/schema/#format">=
Format</a></h3>
<p>The seven primitive types are just that, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Primitive_data_type">primitive</a>, so how do you define more com=
plex value types? One of the ways to do that is using the <code>format</cod=
e> keyword. The <code>format</code> keyword allows for defining additional =
semantic-level validation of values that have a well-defined structure.</p>
<p>For example, to require a date value, you would use the <code>date-time<=
/code> format.</p>
<pre class=3D"language-php" tabindex=3D"0"><code class=3D"language-php"><sp=
an class=3D"token php language-php"><span class=3D"token keyword">array</sp=
an><span class=3D"token punctuation">(</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'type'</span>   <span=
 class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token string single=
-quoted-string">'string'</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'format'</span> <span=
 class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token string single=
-quoted-string">'date-time'</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span=
>
<span class=3D"token punctuation">)</span><span class=3D"token punctuation"=
>;</span>
</span></code></pre>
<p>WordPress supports the following formats:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>date-time</code> A date formatted according to <a href=3D"https:/=
/tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3339#section-5.8">RFC3339</a>.</li>
<li><code>uri</code> A uri according to <a href=3D"https://developer.wordpr=
ess.org/reference/functions/esc_url_raw/"><code>esc_url_raw</code></a>.</li=
>
<li><code>email</code> An email address according to <a href=3D"https://dev=
eloper.wordpress.org/reference/functions/is_email/"><code>is_email</code></=
a>.</li>
<li><code>ip</code> A v4 or v6 ip address.</li>
<li><code>uuid</code> A uuid of any version.</li>
<li><code>hex-color</code> A 3 or 6 character hex color with a leading <cod=
e>#</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>A value must match its format even if the value is an empty string. If a=
llowing an =E2=80=9Cempty=E2=80=9D value is desired, add <code>null</code> =
as a possible type.</p>
<p>For example, the following schema would allow <code>127.0.0.1</code> or =
<code>null</code> as possible values.</p>
<pre class=3D"language-php" tabindex=3D"0"><code class=3D"language-php"><sp=
an class=3D"token php language-php"><span class=3D"token keyword">array</sp=
an><span class=3D"token punctuation">(</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'type'</span>   <span=
 class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token keyword">arra=
y</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">(</span> <span class=3D"token str=
ing single-quoted-string">'string'</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">=
,</span> <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'null'</span> <s=
pan class=3D"token punctuation">)</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,=
</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'format'</span> <span=
 class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token string single=
-quoted-string">'ip'</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
<span class=3D"token punctuation">)</span><span class=3D"token punctuation"=
>;</span>
</span></code></pre>
<h3 id=3D"strings" class=3D"is-toc-heading" tabindex=3D"-1"><a href=3D"http=
s://developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/extending-the-rest-api/schema/#strings=
">Strings</a></h3>
<p>The <code>string</code> types supports three additional keywords.</p>
<h4 id=3D"minlength-and-maxlength" class=3D"is-toc-heading" tabindex=3D"-1"=
><a href=3D"https://developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/extending-the-rest-api=
/schema/#minlength-and-maxlength">minLength and maxLength</a></h4>
<p>The <code>minLength</code> and <code>maxLength</code> keywords can be us=
ed to constrain the acceptable length of a string. Importantly multi-byte c=
haracters are counted as a single character and bounds are inclusive.</p>
<p>For instance, given the following schema, <code>ab</code>, <code>abc</co=
de>, and <code>abcd</code> are valid, while <code>a</code>, and <code>abcde=
</code> are invalid.</p>
<pre class=3D"language-php" tabindex=3D"0"><code class=3D"language-php"><sp=
an class=3D"token php language-php"><span class=3D"token keyword">array</sp=
an><span class=3D"token punctuation">(</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'type'</span>      <s=
pan class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token string sin=
gle-quoted-string">'string'</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span=
>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'minLength'</span> <s=
pan class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token number">2<=
/span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'maxLength'</span> <s=
pan class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token number">4<=
/span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
<span class=3D"token punctuation">)</span><span class=3D"token punctuation"=
>;</span>
</span></code></pre>
<p>The <code>exclusiveMinimum</code> and <code>exclusiveMaximum</code> keyw=
ords do not apply, they are only valid for numbers.</p>
<h4 id=3D"pattern" class=3D"is-toc-heading" tabindex=3D"-1"><a href=3D"http=
s://developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/extending-the-rest-api/schema/#pattern=
">pattern</a></h4>
<p>The JSON Schema keyword <code>pattern</code> can be used to validate tha=
t a string field matches a regular expression.</p>
<p>For instance, given the following schema, <code>#123</code> would be val=
id, but <code>#abc</code> would not.</p>
<pre class=3D"language-php" tabindex=3D"0"><code class=3D"language-php"><sp=
an class=3D"token php language-php"><span class=3D"token keyword">array</sp=
an><span class=3D"token punctuation">(</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'type'</span>    <spa=
n class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token string singl=
e-quoted-string">'string'</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'pattern'</span> <spa=
n class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token string singl=
e-quoted-string">'#[0-9]+'</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
<span class=3D"token punctuation">)</span><span class=3D"token punctuation"=
>;</span>
</span></code></pre>
<p>The regex is not automatically anchored. Regex flags, for instance <code=
>/i</code> to make the match case insensitive are not supported.</p>
<p>The JSON Schema RFC recommends limiting yourself to the following regex =
features so the schema can be interoperable between as many different progr=
amming languages as possible.</p>
<ul>
<li>individual Unicode characters, as defined by the JSON specification [RF=
C4627].</li>
<li>simple character classes <code>[abc]</code>, range character classes <c=
ode>[a-z]</code>.</li>
<li>complemented character classes <code>[^abc]</code>, <code>[^a-z]</code>=
.</li>
<li>simple quantifiers: <code>+</code> (one or more), <code>*</code> (zero =
or more), <code>?</code> (zero or one), and their lazy versions <code>+?</c=
ode>, <code>*?</code>, <code>??</code>.</li>
<li>range quantifiers: <code>{x}</code> (exactly x occurrences), <code>{x,y=
}</code> (at least x, at most y, occurrences), <code>{x,}</code> (x occurre=
nces or more), and their lazy versions.</li>
<li>the beginning-of-input <code>^</code> and end-of-input <code>$</code> a=
nchors.</li>
<li>simple grouping <code>(...)</code> and alternation <code>|</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The pattern should be valid according to the ECMA 262 regex dialect.</p>
<h3 id=3D"numbers" class=3D"is-toc-heading" tabindex=3D"-1"><a href=3D"http=
s://developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/extending-the-rest-api/schema/#numbers=
">Numbers</a></h3>
<p>The <code>number</code> and <code>integer</code> types support four addi=
tional keywords.</p>
<h4 id=3D"minimum-and-maximum" class=3D"is-toc-heading" tabindex=3D"-1"><a =
href=3D"https://developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/extending-the-rest-api/sch=
ema/#minimum-and-maximum">minimum and maximum</a></h4>
<p>The <code>minimum</code> and <code>maximum</code> keyword allow constrai=
ning the range of acceptable numbers. For example, <code>2</code> would be =
valid according to this schema, but <code>0</code> and <code>4</code> would=
 not be.</p>
<pre class=3D"language-php" tabindex=3D"0"><code class=3D"language-php"><sp=
an class=3D"token php language-php"><span class=3D"token keyword">array</sp=
an><span class=3D"token punctuation">(</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'type'</span> <span c=
lass=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token string single-q=
uoted-string">'integer'</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'minimum'</span> <spa=
n class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token number">1</s=
pan><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'maximum'</span> <spa=
n class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token number">3</s=
pan><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
<span class=3D"token punctuation">)</span><span class=3D"token punctuation"=
>;</span>
</span></code></pre>
<p>JSON Schema also allows using the <code>exclusiveMinimum</code> and <cod=
e>exclusiveMaximum</code> keywords to denote that the value cannot equal th=
e defined <code>minimum</code> or <code>maximum</code> respectively. For ex=
ample, in this case only <code>2</code> would be an acceptable value.</p>
<pre class=3D"language-php" tabindex=3D"0"><code class=3D"language-php"><sp=
an class=3D"token php language-php"><span class=3D"token keyword">array</sp=
an><span class=3D"token punctuation">(</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'type'</span>        =
     <span class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token str=
ing single-quoted-string">'integer'</span><span class=3D"token punctuation"=
>,</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'minimum'</span>     =
     <span class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token num=
ber">1</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'exclusiveMinimum'</s=
pan> <span class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token con=
stant boolean">true</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'maximum'</span>     =
     <span class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token num=
ber">3</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'exclusiveMaximum'</s=
pan> <span class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token con=
stant boolean">true</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
<span class=3D"token punctuation">)</span><span class=3D"token punctuation"=
>;</span>
</span></code></pre>
<h4 id=3D"multipleof" class=3D"is-toc-heading" tabindex=3D"-1"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/extending-the-rest-api/schema/#mult=
ipleof">multipleOf</a></h4>
<p>The <code>multipleOf</code> keyword allows for asserting an integer or n=
umber type is a multiple of the given number. For example, this schema will=
 only accept even integers.</p>
<pre class=3D"language-php" tabindex=3D"0"><code class=3D"language-php"><sp=
an class=3D"token php language-php"><span class=3D"token keyword">array</sp=
an><span class=3D"token punctuation">(</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'type'</span>       <=
span class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token string si=
ngle-quoted-string">'integer'</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</sp=
an>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'multipleOf'</span> <=
span class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token number">2=
</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
<span class=3D"token punctuation">)</span><span class=3D"token punctuation"=
>;</span>
</span></code></pre>
<p><code>multipleOf</code> also supports decimals. For example, this schema=
 could be used to accept a percentage with a maximum of 1 decimal point.</p=
>
<pre class=3D"language-php" tabindex=3D"0"><code class=3D"language-php"><sp=
an class=3D"token php language-php"><span class=3D"token keyword">array</sp=
an><span class=3D"token punctuation">(</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'type'</span>       <=
span class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token string si=
ngle-quoted-string">'number'</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</spa=
n>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'minimum'</span>    <=
span class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token number">0=
</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'maximum'</span>    <=
span class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token number">1=
00</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
    <span class=3D"token string single-quoted-string">'multipleOf'</span> <=
span class=3D"token operator">=3D&gt;</span> <span class=3D"token number">0=
.1</span><span class=3D"token punctuation">,</span>
<span class=3D"token punctuation">)</span><span class=3D"token punctuation"=
>;</span>
</span></code></pre>
<h3 id=3D"arrays" class=3D"is-toc-heading" tabindex=3D"-1"><a href=3D"https=
://developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/extending-the-rest-api/schema/#arrays">=
Arrays</a></h3>
<p>Specifying a <code>type</code> of <code>array</code> requires data to be=
